# Docs rules

1. Plain English first; the glossary is for absolute need. Write every doc so a cold reader
   understands it without the glossary. A term earns an entry only when both hold: plain English
   can't carry it without a paragraph of explanation each time, and more than one file uses it.
   Before adding an entry, rewrite the sentence without the term; if the rewrite reads fine, there
   is no entry. A new entry needs {{owner_name}}'s approval, named individually. Removing an entry
   beats adding one.
2. The glossary is the only place a term that passes that test is defined; other files use it and
   never redefine it.
3. A reference is a slug (a decision record by the part of its name after the date, an open
   question or a gate by its heading), never a file path and never code. Cite it as See `slug`,
   and say in the same sentence what it is and why it matters.
4. Current state and history never share a file. Decision records are dated and append-only, with
   Decided, Rejected, and Reopens if; the standing docs hold only what is true now, with no dates,
   status, or rejected alternatives. Verbatim briefs, handoffs, and interviews are filed under
   decision sources and cited by the record that acted on them.
5. One role per file, one fact in one place. If a fact seems to need two homes, one of them cites
   the other.
6. Never write a compressed handle ("the three readers", "lane B", "the sync layer"). Expand it into
   the concrete thing each time.
7. Before asking {{owner_name}} to approve a docs change, run the plugin's canon check and report
   each PASS or FAIL; a failure is fixed and rerun first.
